WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.246 --> 00:00:03.817
- Okay, so, I am much older
than my coworker Eric,

00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:06.839
but sometimes that just
means I'm much wiser.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:08.114
For example, Eric thought:

00:00:08.114 --> 00:00:09.458
Say what you just said again.

00:00:09.458 --> 00:00:11.936
- Paris Hilton invented the selfie.

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:12.866
- She didn't invent the selfie.

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:13.699
- Oh my God, yes.

00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:14.532
Look, look.

00:00:14.532 --> 00:00:16.131
Huh? Huh?

00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:17.893
Eleven years ago today.

00:00:17.893 --> 00:00:18.726
- [Sarah] Okay.

00:00:18.726 --> 00:00:20.602
- [Eric] Paris Hilton and
Britney invented the selfie.

00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:21.758
There, they're taking the selfie.

00:00:21.758 --> 00:00:25.026
- But in 2004, the movie
"13 Going on 30" came out,

00:00:25.026 --> 00:00:27.477
and that movie has a scene
where they take a selfie.

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:29.473
- I've never even heard of
that movie, so I doubt it.

00:00:29.473 --> 00:00:32.140
- Hold on. I'm going to show it to you.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.463
- [Eric] That's a camera though.

00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:36.826
- [Sarah] That's like
a selfie from 2004, so.

00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:39.206
- I was doing work and now
you got me invested in this,

00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:40.942
and I need to know like who actually

00:00:40.942 --> 00:00:42.339
invented the first selfie.

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:43.186
- I'm gunna find out.

00:00:43.186 --> 00:00:44.317
- I need you to find this.

00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:45.855
- Ten minutes, baby.

00:00:45.855 --> 00:00:46.753
- I'm going to watch this movie for now.

00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:47.922
- Wait, you're watching the movie?

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:49.782
- Ya, it seems really good, to be honest.

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:52.949
(upbeat techno music)

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.323
- I had to find out who
invented the selfie.

00:00:59.323 --> 00:01:01.638
I mean I work at BuzzFeed, so
it's like my job to do that.

00:01:01.638 --> 00:01:03.696
It's not my job to do that specifically,

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.567
but I made it my job, today at least.

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:09.757
So, I called BuzzFeed's
resident selfie expert.

00:01:09.757 --> 00:01:10.590
Hey, Jazz!

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:15.044
- Taking selfies is like really important

00:01:15.044 --> 00:01:17.169
to make sure that you're
confident when you do it

00:01:17.169 --> 00:01:19.655
because, you know, everybody's
going to be seeing this.

00:01:19.655 --> 00:01:22.460
You want to make sure you
find some good lighting.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.130
- Okay, so who?

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.102
- And like it's always good if you like

00:01:26.102 --> 00:01:28.322
match your background, or like contrast

00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:29.599
with your background, you know,

00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:31.181
like play off the colors.

00:01:31.181 --> 00:01:33.523
- So who invented the selfie?

00:01:33.523 --> 00:01:36.846
- I would assume Paris
Hilton, but I'm not sure.

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:38.404
- That's not who really invented it.

00:01:38.404 --> 00:01:41.585
Like who really invented the
selfie, like the first person?

00:01:41.585 --> 00:01:44.427
- Is that what you wanted
to know the whole time?

00:01:44.427 --> 00:01:47.344
You could literally just Google it.

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:51.548
Just go on, type on your little keyboard,

00:01:51.548 --> 00:01:53.198
"Who invented the selfie"?

00:01:53.198 --> 00:01:55.312
- So I decided to Google it.

00:01:55.312 --> 00:01:57.835
Turns out, there's not
really a definitive answer,

00:01:57.835 --> 00:01:59.773
at least not on the first like two pages

00:01:59.773 --> 00:02:01.351
of my Google search results.

00:02:01.351 --> 00:02:03.852
Not only did Paris
Hilton have nothing to do

00:02:03.852 --> 00:02:06.125
with inventing the selfie,
she didn't even coin the term.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:09.686
The first documented use
of the term "selfie,"

00:02:09.686 --> 00:02:10.878
at least, in print,

00:02:10.878 --> 00:02:14.830
was in September 2002
when a user "Hopey" posted

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.974
a close-up of his busted lip, writing,

00:02:16.974 --> 00:02:18.934
"I had a hole about one centimeter long,

00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:20.317
right through my bottom lip.

00:02:20.317 --> 00:02:23.737
And sorry about the
focus, it was a selfie."

00:02:23.737 --> 00:02:26.624
Christian, did you see my slack?

00:02:26.624 --> 00:02:28.115
It's the first selfie.

00:02:28.115 --> 00:02:28.948
Cool, right?

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:29.839
- That's not a selfie.

00:02:29.839 --> 00:02:32.080
- It doesn't look like
a selfie as we know it,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.745
but it's just the lips, but it's still.

00:02:33.745 --> 00:02:38.570
- Okay, so, Hopey himself says,
"It was not a word I coined.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.326
It's something that was just
common slang at the time,

00:02:41.326 --> 00:02:43.753
used to describe a picture of yourself."

00:02:43.753 --> 00:02:45.779
- Okay, well then who coined the term?

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:47.535
- Coining it is different
than inventing it.

00:02:47.535 --> 00:02:48.368
Think about it.

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:51.036
Like artists for centuries
were doing self portraits,

00:02:51.036 --> 00:02:53.322
since like before the 1500s.

00:02:53.322 --> 00:02:55.213
- Should have paid attention in art class.

00:02:55.213 --> 00:02:57.827
According to James Hall,
people in the 15th century

00:02:57.827 --> 00:03:00.091
were manipulating their self-images,

00:03:00.091 --> 00:03:02.493
sometimes making
themselves appear younger,

00:03:02.493 --> 00:03:05.281
and sometimes casting
themselves in biblical roles

00:03:05.281 --> 00:03:07.550
because branding is, after all,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.282
the second oldest profession.

00:03:09.282 --> 00:03:11.591
More so than, Hopey's busted lip,

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.814
these self portraits with
their manipulated images

00:03:14.814 --> 00:03:17.410
capture the spirit of the selfie.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.387
Albrecht Durer, a self-portraitist

00:03:19.387 --> 00:03:21.078
and a well known narcissist,

00:03:21.078 --> 00:03:23.859
used to paint himself
with long, flowing hair

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:25.678
to appear more Christ like,

00:03:25.678 --> 00:03:28.430
although his actual
appearance is more reminiscent

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.414
of a guy from the early 90s
with over-plucked eyebrows

00:03:31.414 --> 00:03:34.027
and permed hair, but honestly,

00:03:34.027 --> 00:03:36.387
what's wrong with a little
selfie manipulation?

00:03:36.387 --> 00:03:38.343
When I post a selfie, my teeth are white,

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:42.890
my eyes are big, and my nose
is practically non-existent.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.125
I'm basically a Bratz doll.

00:03:45.125 --> 00:03:48.565
Rembrandt, Courbet, Van Gogh,
they all painted selfies.

00:03:48.565 --> 00:03:50.797
Sadly, a few hours into
the middle of my research,

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.835
I hit a bit of a snag.

00:03:52.835 --> 00:03:55.428
It's between Rembrandt,
Courbet, and Van Gogh.

00:03:55.428 --> 00:03:57.104
- Before you do that,
we have to figure out

00:03:57.104 --> 00:03:59.466
if like paintings are selfies.

00:03:59.466 --> 00:04:01.687
Like have you looked at a
definition of what a selfie is?

00:04:01.687 --> 00:04:04.106
- Ya, I looked at, of course
I looked the definition.

00:04:04.106 --> 00:04:05.939
- So, what did it say?

00:04:07.926 --> 00:04:09.906
- I didn't look at a definition.

00:04:09.906 --> 00:04:13.206
- Alright, so let's look up
the definition real quick.

00:04:13.206 --> 00:04:14.091
- Alright, good.

00:04:14.091 --> 00:04:16.453
- According to Oxford it
is a photograph that one

00:04:16.453 --> 00:04:19.463
has taken of oneself, typically
with a smartphone or webcam

00:04:19.463 --> 00:04:22.629
and uploaded to a social media website.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.526
- A selfie has to be a photo.

00:04:24.526 --> 00:04:28.244
Everything I've done to this
point has been pointless.

00:04:28.244 --> 00:04:30.182
- How much time did you put into this?

00:04:30.182 --> 00:04:32.157
- We're going to have
to time travel forward

00:04:32.157 --> 00:04:35.190
a couple hundred years to
the invention of the camera.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.240
The first partially successful
images were produced

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.593
around 1816 by Nicéphore Niépce,

00:04:40.593 --> 00:04:42.875
but those negatives didn't even last long.

00:04:42.875 --> 00:04:44.515
He used silver chloride,

00:04:44.515 --> 00:04:46.627
which darkens when exposed to light.

00:04:46.627 --> 00:04:48.708
Those images disappeared.

00:04:48.708 --> 00:04:51.586
He was basically the
19th century SnapChat.

00:04:51.586 --> 00:04:54.111
So, we can't really say for
sure whether Nicéphore Niépce

00:04:54.111 --> 00:04:55.923
took that that first selfie

00:04:55.923 --> 00:04:58.113
because we don't have the photos anymore.

00:04:58.113 --> 00:05:00.179
The Library of Congress believes

00:05:00.179 --> 00:05:02.037
the first selfie taken in America

00:05:02.037 --> 00:05:06.340
was a photographic portrait
by Robert Cornelius in 1839.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.339
Robert had to hold still for 15 minutes

00:05:08.339 --> 00:05:10.139
to get the perfect selfie,

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:13.525
which is not too different
than how long it takes

00:05:13.525 --> 00:05:15.245
to get the perfect selfie today,

00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:19.412
since we take like 80 photos
on average, maybe 100.

00:05:20.481 --> 00:05:21.314
Come here.

00:05:21.314 --> 00:05:22.147
- You got the answer?

00:05:22.147 --> 00:05:23.136
- Ya, come here.

00:05:23.136 --> 00:05:25.019
That's my main man, Robert C. from Philly,

00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:28.153
and he took the first selfie in America,

00:05:28.153 --> 00:05:29.268
and that's it right there.

00:05:29.268 --> 00:05:30.238
- Where's the camera?

00:05:30.238 --> 00:05:32.410
- Well he had to like set it,

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.242
and then he had to run around and go.

00:05:34.242 --> 00:05:36.870
Selfie sticks, that was not a thing.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.065
- He used an Instagram
filter and like took credit

00:05:39.065 --> 00:05:39.898
for inventing the selfie.

00:05:39.898 --> 00:05:40.788
- [Sarah] He didn't.

00:05:40.788 --> 00:05:42.319
- It's called a colloidal wet plate.

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:43.546
- That's the filter?

00:05:43.546 --> 00:05:45.618
- Paris Hilton is not even
the first celeb selfie

00:05:45.618 --> 00:05:49.081
because Anastasia Romanov
took that in 1914.

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:50.401
- [Christian] Is that a duck face?

00:05:50.401 --> 00:05:51.709
- So, she invented the selfie?

00:05:51.709 --> 00:05:54.129
- Rob from Philly invented the selfie.

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:56.110
- Ya, well I wouldn't say he invented

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.836
the selfie either because actually.

00:05:58.836 --> 00:06:02.143
The selfie isn't really an
invention; it's a phenomenon.

00:06:02.143 --> 00:06:04.694
The inventor isn't a particular person,

00:06:04.694 --> 00:06:07.803
it's the Internet, social media,

00:06:07.803 --> 00:06:10.210
our own vain narcissistic selves.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.177
And goddammit, we don't want
to waste our time waiting

00:06:13.177 --> 00:06:15.102
for someone else to take it.

00:06:15.102 --> 00:06:18.393
We can take the photo our God damn selves.

00:06:18.393 --> 00:06:21.313
So, who invented the selfie?

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:24.172
It's you; you invented the selfie.

00:06:24.172 --> 00:06:25.444
- No, no, stop.

00:06:25.444 --> 00:06:26.344
I don't want it.

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:27.177
I didn't invent the selfie.

00:06:27.177 --> 00:06:28.958
I don't want to start any drama.

00:06:28.958 --> 00:06:29.813
- Like capital "you."

00:06:29.813 --> 00:06:31.536
- The greater sense of "you."

00:06:31.536 --> 00:06:32.369
- Yes, ya, thank you.

00:06:32.369 --> 00:06:35.331
Like, ya, humanity, we all did together.

00:06:35.331 --> 00:06:37.883
It's like a phenomenon; it's not a person.

00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:42.050
- Is that the ending of this,
is you just copping out?

00:06:43.277 --> 00:06:45.311
- It felt deep when I wrote it.

00:06:45.311 --> 00:06:46.199
- This is for a video?

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:47.541
- Ya, it's for a video.

00:06:47.541 --> 00:06:48.875
- We are running out of ideas.

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:49.875
- Oh my God.

00:06:53.241 --> 00:06:56.935
I guess I didn't really
answer the question.

00:06:56.935 --> 00:07:01.161
- [Eric] I just made 20
bucks off of Bitcoin.

00:07:01.161 --> 00:07:03.911
(dramatic music)

